## Best Idea Wins (Universal Gate)

Every change must satisfy all three:

1. **Working** — runs end-to-end on a stated case (scenario or test).
2. **Fixable** — small surface, inspectable state, next-week-you can debug it.
3. **Explainable** — design choice defended in plain language in the PR description.

If you can't defend all three, do not open the PR. Iterate first.

Hierarchy is flat: best idea wins regardless of who proposed it.

## Real-World Inspiration vs Constraint

Computer science, math, physics, anatomy, neuroscience are **scaffolding**,
not constraints. Luna runs in binary; we choose the parameters. Borrow the
*shapes* of ideas freely (activation, reinforcement, plastic edges). Do not
hide implementation truth behind metaphor — describe what the code actually
does in plain terms.

---

## Mechanically Enforced (Build Will Reject)

### CI Gates (`.github/workflows/doctrine.yml`)

Every push/PR runs `cargo test --workspace`, `bash scripts/doctrine_check.sh`,
and every `scenarios/runtime/*.json` against `luna-cli runtime scenario`.
Red build = no merge.

### Doctrine Lint (`scripts/doctrine_check.sh`)

Forbidden patterns in production crate code:
- `if x == "Joe"`, `match x { "Chris" => ... }` — entity-name dispatch.
  Tests are fine; **control flow dispatching on a hardcoded scenario name is
  the Aura failure mode** and will fail the build.
- `scenarios/runtime/` going empty.

### Type-System Gates (Compile-Time Impossible)

- **`luna_core::RecallReason`** — strict newtype on `String`.
  - `RecallReason::new("")` returns `Err`. Empty/whitespace-only is rejected.
  - `RecallHit.reason` and `EpisodeRecalled.reason` are `RecallReason`.
  - **You cannot construct surfaced memory without explaining why.**
  - Deserialization is lenient: legacy empty strings load as `<unrecorded>`.
  - Use `RecallReason::new("...").expect("static recall reason is non-empty")`
    for static call sites.

### PR Template (`.github/pull_request_template.md`)

Every PR must fill:
- Best Idea Check (working / fixable / explainable boxes)
- Memory Doctrine Check (failure mode, mechanism, scenario)
- Hardcoding Review (no scripted facts/answers)
- Memory Architecture (event log truth, tier preservation)
- Tests (workspace, scenarios, doctrine check)
- Doctrine Revision (link if relaxing a rule, else N/A)

